::GEO_U02_L07_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A reflection followed by a translation maps triangle ABC onto triangle DEF. What follows? {
=A. Triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF#Correct. A sequence of rigid motions supports congruence.
~B. Triangle ABC is larger.#Rigid motions do not change size.
~C. The triangles are similar but not congruent.#Rigid motions preserve exact size and shape.
~D. No conclusion can be made.#The mapping gives enough evidence for congruence.
}

::GEO_U02_L07_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.5<br><br>Question:<br>In a two-step transformation, what is transformed second? {
~A. The original preimage again.#This ignores the first transformation.
=B. The image from the first transformation.#Correct. The output of step one becomes the input for step two.
~C. Only the longest side.#Every vertex must be transformed.
~D. Only one vertex.#A polygon image needs all vertices.
}

::GEO_U02_L07_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Why does a rigid transformation justify congruence? {
~A. It changes side lengths by a scale factor.#Scaling changes size.
~B. It makes figures close together.#Location does not prove congruence.
=C. It preserves distance and angle measure.#Correct. Congruence requires same size and shape.
~D. It changes shape but keeps color.#Color is not a geometric property.
}

::GEO_U02_L07_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.5<br><br>Question:<br>A(1, -2) is reflected across the x-axis, then translated 3 units right. What is A&quot;? {
~A. (4, -2)#This skips the reflection.
~B. (-2, 1)#This swaps coordinates.
~C. (1, 5)#This does not match either transformation.
=D. (4, 2)#Correct. Reflect to (1, 2), then translate to (4, 2).
}

::GEO_U02_L07_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly justifies congruence? {
=A. A sequence of rigid transformations maps one figure exactly onto the other.#Correct. Exact mapping by rigid motions proves congruence.
~B. The figures are the same color.#Color is not mathematical evidence.
~C. The figures appear close in size.#Appearance is not enough.
~D. One figure is a dilation by scale factor 2.#A scale factor of 2 changes size.
}

